PLACES=[
N('lemmon-petrified-wood-park','Lemmon Petrified Wood Park and Museum','Lemmon','sd','landmark','standing',45.93947,-102.15861,'site','open','walk','','1930–1933',
  'A roadside park of towers, spires and buildings made of petrified wood, begun in 1930 by Ole Quammen, a lumberman and one-time mayor of Lemmon, who had collected fossils across Perkins County. Up to 40 men worked on it in the Depression, one team hauling stone by wagon and one building to his plans; the museum was finished late in 1933. It sat on the Yellowstone Trail, was listed on the National Register in 1977 as the Lemmon Petrified Park, and claimed to be the largest petrified wood park in the world.',
  'About a hundred cones and towers of stacked logs and cannonball concretions, from 8 to 32 feet high; the octagonal Castle at the centre, built of 300 tons of material with dinosaur, snake and bird tracks in its surfaces; the museum building, itself of petrified wood (a second wing was added in 1989); and a disused gas station of the same stone. Some material has been lost to theft: about 3,200 of the original 4,100 tons of wood remain (Wikipedia). Admission is free.',
  'A Depression town built a park of 4,000 tons of stone trees.',
  accn='Run by the town of Lemmon; 500 Main Avenue, off US 12. The town\'s attractions page gives the museum\'s season and hours and says it is free; street parking. Hours read November 2026.',
  links=[W('Lemmon Petrified Wood Park & Museum'),['Lemmon SD: attractions','https://lemmonsd.squarespace.com/attractions-1']],
  brick='no',hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Memorial Day to Labor Day'),
N('scherr-howe-auditorium-mobridge','Mobridge Auditorium (Scherr-Howe Event Center) and the Oscar Howe murals','Mobridge','sd','civic','standing',45.53473,-100.433448,'exact','open','drive','','1936–1937',
  'The city auditorium, a WPA project of 1936 designed by Roland Wilcken, opened on May 6, 1937. In 1942 the young Dakota artist Oscar Howe, working under his mentor Andre Boratko, painted ten murals of Dakota life and history on the hall\'s walls; he had been inducted into the Army in June and was given a furlough to finish them (the city\'s pages give 12 and 14 days). The building was listed on the National Register in 1986 and the murals were restored in 2014.',
  'An art deco front of tan brick under a metal canopy, the sides in dark brown brick (Register form), and inside the gymnasium-auditorium the ten murals, among them the Fool Soldier Rescue, a Sun Dance, Sacagawea with Lewis and Clark and Treaty Making (city of Mobridge). The murals are open to the public.',
  'Oscar Howe\'s earliest murals, painted on an Army furlough.',
  accn='City of Mobridge, 212 North Main Street; street parking on Main. Visitors check in at the Chamber of Commerce in the same building. The city\'s two pages give the hours as 8 a.m. and as 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., weekdays; call 605-845-3555 first. Read November 2026.',
  links=[NOM('86001189'),['City of Mobridge: Scherr-Howe Event Center','https://mobridge.org/scherr-howe-event-center'],['Mobridge: Oscar Howe murals','https://mobridge.org/murals']],
  brick='no',hours='Monday to Friday, 8 or 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (the city\'s pages differ); check in at the Chamber of Commerce'),
N('selby-opera-house','Selby Opera House','Selby','sd','civic','standing',45.50607,-100.0326,'site','view','drive','','1909–',
  'A two-storey red brick hall of 1909 on Main Street, built for touring shows and town gatherings. The Register form of 1987 says it had served for over 75 years as the main place of entertainment for Selby and the country round it; the auditorium was enlarged in the 1940s. The City of Selby owned it when it was listed in 1987.',
  'A red brick Classical Revival front (photographs on Wikimedia Commons) with a projecting dentil cornice, stone lintels and sills to every window and a stone lintel over the double doors at the top of four concrete steps; a gambrel roof. Inside, a two-storey auditorium with a stage, which kept its original hardwood floors in 1987 (Register form). Present use and opening not checked; look from Main Street.',
  '',
  accn='Owned by the City of Selby in 1987 (Register form); present owner and use not checked. 3409 Main Street; street parking.',
  links=[W('Selby Opera House'),NOM('87001730')],
  brick='yes',hours=''),
N('verendrye-site-fort-pierre','Verendrye Site (lead plate hill)','Fort Pierre','sd','curio','marker',44.35556,-100.37861,'site','public','walk','','1743',
  'On this low hill above the Missouri the La Vérendrye brothers, the first Europeans known in the region, buried a lead plate with the arms of France on March 30, 1743, to claim the country for their king. Children playing on the eroding hillside found the plate on February 16, 1913; the State Historical Society holds it. The site was made a National Historic Landmark in 1991 and the city keeps it as a small park.',
  'A granite marker about 4 feet high, set up in 1933 by the State Historical Society and the Fort Pierre Commercial Club, telling the story of the plate; the view east over the Missouri to Pierre (Wikipedia).',
  'Children found France\'s claim to Dakota in a hillside in 1913.',
  accn='A city park reached by a paved drive off Verendrye Drive, with a small turnaround and a few parking spaces beside the monument (Wikipedia). Open ground; no hours were found.',
  links=[W('Verendrye Site')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('shakespeare-garden-wessington-springs','Shakespeare Garden and Anne Hathaway Cottage','Wessington Springs','sd','landmark','standing',44.0844,-98.57545,'site','open','walk','','1927–1932',
  'Emma Shay, an English teacher at Wessington Springs College, toured English gardens in 1926 and had her class plant a Shakespeare garden in the spring of 1927, some of it from seed she brought from England. Her retirement house, finished in 1932, copies Anne Hathaway\'s cottage at Shottery. After the college closed the grounds declined; in 1989 a Shakespeare Garden Society bought and restored them and had the roof thatched, as the Shays had first planned. It was listed on the National Register in 1979 as the Shakespeare Garden and Shay House.',
  'A two-storey stucco cottage with a steep thatched roof, said to be the only thatched building in South Dakota; the garden of over 150 kinds of flowers, trees and shrubs, with a flagstone walk through stone-edged beds to a lattice summerhouse, a covered gate and picket fence, and stone and concrete urns and bench (Register form, HMdb, Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'The only thatched roof in South Dakota.',
  accn='Owned by the Shakespeare Garden Society (Wikipedia). On Alene Avenue North / 6th Street North on the west side of town, where the two markers stand; street parking. The Wessington Springs chamber\'s page says the garden is open in daylight and the cottage is shown by appointment through the chamber (605-539-1929). Read November 2026.',
  links=[W('Shakespeare Garden and Anne Hathaway Cottage'),NOM('79003681'),HM('113051','Shakespeare Garden/Ann Hathaway Cottage'),HM('113052','The Shakespeare Garden'),['Wessington Springs: historical tours','https://wessingtonsprings.com/historical-tours']],
  brick='no',hours='Garden open in daylight; cottage by appointment through the Wessington Springs chamber'),
N('hawkeye-valley-mill','Hawkeye Valley Mill','Wessington Springs','sd','industry','standing',43.972025,-98.591658,'area','view','drive','','1894–',
  'A farm feed mill with its windmill tower built inside a two-storey wooden barn, put up in 1894 by Elwood C. Lyle, who had homesteaded the quarter-section in 1889, in the Hawkeye Valley of the Wessington Hills. It shelled corn, ground feed, sharpened tools and pumped water for his farm and his neighbours. Its first wooden Challenge wheel was replaced in the late 1930s or 1940s by a steel Monitor wheel, when it became a pump mill. Listed on the National Register in 2012 as a rare survivor of the small wind-powered farm mills of the plains.',
  'The weathered barn with the timber windmill tower rising through it and the broken remnant of the Monitor wheel and gear box at the top; doors are missing and part of the machinery shows in an opening below the north gable (Register form, 2012). Most of the machinery inside is gone.',
  'A windmill built inside a barn.',
  accn='Private farmland in Anina Township south of Wessington Springs, west of Firesteel Creek (Register form). Look only from the public road; do not go onto the farm. Exact spot not confirmed (see the pin note).',
  links=[NOM('12001216')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('hollands-grist-mill-milbank','Hollands Grist Mill (Milbank windmill)','Milbank','sd','industry','standing',45.22222,-96.61919,'exact','public','walk','','1884–1910',
  'An English-style smock windmill built by Henry Hollands, who came from England in 1870; the Register form gives 1884, a state public broadcaster\'s piece 1885. It ground corn and wheat, 40 to 50 bushels an hour, and sawed wood; local tradition says it stopped about 1910 because shelter belts round the farms had cut the wind. Civic groups bought it in 1912; it was moved into a city park in 1928 and, after a windstorm in 1977, to the grounds of the Milbank Mutual Insurance Company. It was rebuilt from 1997 and finished in 2009 (SDPB; southdakota.com) and now stands in Mill Park.',
  'A six-sided wooden mill 44 feet high, 24 feet across at the base narrowing to 12, with four sails seven by thirty feet; in 1981 the grindstones, drive shafts and wooden drive wheels were still inside (Register form). How much of that survived the rebuilding was not checked. A small museum with restrooms stands by it; tours and demonstrations are held several times a year (southdakota.com).',
  'The wind died when the farms planted trees.',
  accn='Mill Park, 4th Avenue and Flynn Drive, Milbank, on US 12 (southdakota.com); the City of Milbank owned the mill when it was listed. Grounds open; park on the park road that runs past the mill on its south side. Tour days not checked.',
  links=[NOM('81000573'),['SDPB: Reconstructing an Icon','https://www.sdpb.org/rural-life-and-history/reconstructing-an-icon-dakota-life'],['SouthDakota.com: Milbank Grist Mill and Mill Park','https://southdakota.com/?p=9152']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours=''),
N('brown-earth-church-stockholm','Brown Earth Presbyterian Church (Old Indian Church)','Stockholm','sd','worship','standing',45.12216,-96.77211,'exact','public','walk','','1877–',
  'In 1875 about thirty Santee Dakota families took homesteads on the prairie of Brown Earth, just south of the Sisseton Reservation, under the American Board of Commissioners for Foreign Missions. In 1877 the Reverend Daniel Renville opened this log church on his own homestead and preached in it for years; it was abandoned in 1884 when most of the families went back to the reservation. Swedish congregations used it from 1892 to 1905, and then the church and two acres were deeded to the county.',
  'A small church of hand-hewn logs with dovetailed corners, about 20 by 25 feet, with an open frame belfry (the bell is missing) and two sash windows on each side; the floor, once dirt, is now concrete, and in 1984 much of the original furniture was still inside (Register form). Renville\'s wife is buried in the little cemetery beside it. A dirt path through prairie grass and pines leads to the door. A marker stands at the church (HMdb). Interior opening not checked.',
  'A log church of the Dakota homesteaders, 1877.',
  accn='Owned by Grant County (Register form). About a mile north and a mile east of Stockholm, on 156th Street half a mile west of 472nd Avenue (HMdb); park on the road shoulder. No hours known.',
  links=[W('Brown Earth Presbyterian Church'),NOM('84003288'),HM('197529','Brown Earth Church')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('stock-judging-pavilion-brookings','Stock Judging Pavilion (South Dakota Agricultural Heritage Museum)','Brookings','sd','inst','standing',44.318964,-96.788917,'exact','open','walk','','1918–1925',
  'Built in 1918 on the State College campus as the livestock teaching hall, with a show ring for animals and concrete seating above the holding pens; a two-storey wing for classrooms and a meat laboratory was added in 1925. A Neo-classical building with 12-inch masonry walls under steel trusses. It became the home of the state\'s Agricultural Heritage Museum and was listed on the National Register in 1978.',
  'The Neo-classical hall and its 1925 wing; the clay tile roof and the cupola and skylights are gone (Register form). Inside, the museum\'s exhibits of farm life, with a steam engine among the large pieces (SDSU). Photographs show red brick walls, an arched entrance and a red roof (Wikimedia Commons). Original interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  accn='South Dakota State University, 977 11th Street at Medary Avenue. Visitor parking lot on the west side of the building; accessible entrance on 11th Street. Hours from the museum\'s own page, read November 2026.',
  links=[NOM('78002538'),['SDSU: Visit the Ag Museum','https://sdstate.edu/south-dakota-agricultural-heritage-museum/visit-ag-museum']],
  brick='yes',hours='Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sunday 1 to 5 p.m. (closed Sundays January to March)'),
N('austin-whittemore-house-vermillion','Austin-Whittemore House','Vermillion','sd','landmark','standing',42.778812,-96.935785,'exact','open','walk','','1884–',
  'A red brick house of 1884 on the bluff above Vermillion, built for Horace Austin, who served several terms in the Dakota legislature and died in office in 1891 (Register form). It was listed on the National Register in 1972 and is now the Clay County Historical Society\'s museum.',
  'A two-storey house of red brick in common bond under a hipped shingled roof, mixing Victorian and plains styles (Register form). Rooms with Clay County furnishings and displays, and a log cabin school on the grounds (Clay County Historical Society).',
  '',
  accn='Clay County Historical Society, 15 Austin Street. Street parking. Hours from the society\'s page, read November 2026; tours outside them by appointment (605-624-8266).',
  links=[W('Austin–Whittemore House'),NOM('72001225'),['Clay County Historical Society: the house museum','https://cchssd.org/austin-whittemore-house-museum/']],
  brick='yes',hours='Monday, Wednesday and Friday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.; other times by appointment'),
N('custer-1881-courthouse-museum','1881 Custer County Courthouse (Courthouse Museum)','Custer','sd','civic','standing',43.766296,-103.601526,'exact','open','walk','','1881–1973',
  'Custer County\'s courthouse of 1881, three storeys of red brick in a plains version of the Italianate style, served the county from Dakota Territory days until 1973. When the county built a new courthouse across the street, the Custer County Historical Society petitioned for the old one and opened it as a museum (Wikipedia gives September 6, 1976; the society\'s page dates the museum from 1975). It was listed on the National Register in 1972.',
  'The red brick courthouse itself, and exhibits on Lakota culture, Custer\'s 1874 expedition and the settlers of the southern Black Hills (Wikipedia). Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  accn='Custer County Historical Society, 411 Mount Rushmore Road. Admission $8 adults, $6 seniors, $4 ages 12 to 17; free for veterans, children 11 and under and county residents. Street parking. Season and hours from the museum\'s own page, read November 2026.',
  links=[W('1881 Courthouse Museum'),W('Custer County Courthouse (South Dakota)'),['Custer County 1881 Courthouse Museum','https://1881courthousemuseum.com/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',hours='May 11 to September 27 (2026): Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('jewel-cave-historic-area','Jewel Cave historic ranger cabin and natural entrance','Custer','sd','landmark','standing',43.733333,-103.841389,'area','open','walk','','1900–1935',
  'Frank and Albert Michaud found a small hole blowing cold air in Hell Canyon in 1900, enlarged it and began showing the cave; it became a national monument in 1908 and passed to the National Park Service in 1933. The Civilian Conservation Corps built the log ranger cabin in 1935; it was the monument\'s only visitor centre and the rangers\' home until the present visitor centre opened in 1972, then decayed until it was restored in 2000. The cabin, trail and entrance are on the National Register.',
  'A small log cabin in NPS rustic style on stone footings with a stone chimney, praised in 1938 as a concentrated essence of log cabin technique (Register form); NPS says the sink, fireplace and kitchen cabinets are original. A quarter-mile walk leads to the cave\'s only known natural entrance, the Michauds\' hole, which the summer Historic Lantern Tour still uses (NPS).',
  'The Michaud brothers\' hole in Hell Canyon.',
  accn='Jewel Cave National Monument (NPS), the Historic Area a mile west of the visitor centre off US 16. No entrance fee; cave entry only by ranger tour, booked on recreation.gov. The Historic Lantern Tour runs in summer (June 1 to August 28 in 2026), is strenuous and has about 500 steep steps. Hours from the NPS hours page, read November 2026.',
  links=[NOM('95000336'),['NPS: Historic Entrance','https://www.nps.gov/places/historic-entrance.htm'],['NPS: Historic Ranger Cabin','https://www.nps.gov/places/historic-ranger-cabin-northern-side.htm'],['NPS: Historic Lantern Tour','https://www.nps.gov/jeca/planyourvisit/lanterntour.htm'],['NPS: hours and seasons','https://www.nps.gov/jeca/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  th=[43.73834,-103.84537],thn='Historic Area / Hell Canyon lot',brick='no',hours='Historic Area Tuesday to Saturday 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., closes late September; lantern tours June to late August'),
N('pig-tail-bridge-wind-cave','Pig Tail Bridge','Wind Cave National Park','sd','landmark','standing',43.60111,-103.49389,'site','public','drive','','about 1930',
  'A spiral bridge built about 1930 when South Dakota Highway 87 was laid out through what is now Wind Cave National Park: the road loops round and passes over itself to gain height. It is 160 feet long and 20 feet wide and was listed on the National Register in 1995.',
  'The road curling over its own bridge; best seen from the loop or the roadside (Wikipedia). Construction material not checked.',
  'The highway that crosses over itself.',
  accn='On SD 87, a public highway, inside Wind Cave National Park (Wikipedia). Pull-offs only where the park provides them; nearest mapped lots are on SD 87 north and south of the bridge.',
  links=[W('Pig Tail Bridge')],
  th=[43.59532,-103.48952],thn='Pull-off on SD 87',brick='?',hours=''),
N('homestake-opera-house-lead','Historic Homestake Opera House','Lead','sd','civic','standing',44.351728,-103.766695,'exact','open','walk','','1912–1914',
  'Built from 1912 to 1914 by the Homestake Mining Company, with money from Phoebe Hearst, as a recreation centre for the miners and their families: a 1,000-seat theatre, a library with books in the miners\' own languages, a bowling alley, billiards, social rooms and a heated swimming pool underground. It opened in 1914 and was given to the city in 1972. On April 2, 1984 a fire that began under the stage gutted the theatre; the main floor was spared. A nonprofit has been restoring the theatre since and holds shows in it.',
  'The recreation building on Main Street; inside, the fire-scarred theatre being restored (500 seats in use), and an upstairs exhibit with 1914 swimming-pool items and the fire-damaged grand chandelier (Deadwood visitor guide). Restoration not checked in detail.',
  'A theatre built by a gold mine, gutted in 1984 and coming back.',
  accn='Historic Homestake Opera House, a nonprofit, 313 West Main Street; street parking. Its own page offers tours by booking. A Deadwood visitor guide lists 30-minute tours at the door Memorial Day to mid-October, Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m., $10 (read November 2026); the opera house\'s own page gives no times.',
  links=[HM('121500','The Homestake Opera House & Recreation Building'),['Historic Homestake Opera House','https://homestakeoperahouse.org/'],['Deadwood: Historic Homestake Opera House','https://www.deadwood.com/business/arts-entertainment/homestake-opera-house-2/'],['SDPB: Homestake Opera House turns 100','https://sdpb.org/news/2014-08-28/leads-historic-homestake-opera-house-turns-100']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
]
